Keep the last video frame across a GStreamer flush on non-RGB buffers.

THE BUG

MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer::flushCurrentBuffer() runs on every GST_EVENT_FLUSH_START
and on every GST_QUERY_DRAIN that reaches the video sink pad (GStreamerVideoSinkCommon.cpp
:64 and :149), i.e. on every DASH quality switch and every V4L2 dynamic-resolution-change
drain. It calls CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferProxy::dropCurrentBufferWhilePreservingTexture(),
which hops to the compositing run loop and calls
CoordinatedPlatformLayer::replaceCurrentContentsBufferWithCopy():

    m_contentsBuffer.pending = nullptr;
    if (is<CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferVideo>(*m_contentsBuffer.committed))
        m_contentsBuffer.pending = downcast<...Video>(*m_contentsBuffer.committed).copyBuffer();
    m_contentsBuffer.committed = WTF::move(m_contentsBuffer.pending);
    ensureTarget().setContentsLayer(m_contentsBuffer.committed.get());

CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferVideo::copyBuffer() bailed out with nullptr unless the inner
buffer was a CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferRGB *wrapping a foreign texture id*. On a device
that decodes with a V4L2 stateful hardware decoder (venus on msm8998, Mesa freedreno a540,
USE(GBM) on), createBufferIfNeeded() never produces that: it produces a DMABuf buffer, or an
ExternalOES / YUV buffer, or no inner buffer at all (a mapped sysmem frame uploaded lazily in
paintToTextureMapper()). So copyBuffer() returned nullptr, setContentsLayer(nullptr) ran, and
TextureMapperLayer::paintSelf() bailed at "if (!contentsLayer) return" -- the video area
painted nothing until the next decoded frame arrived. Symptom: a black flash of the video
rectangle several times per minute on 4K60 YouTube. "dropCurrentBufferWhilePreservingTexture"
preserved no texture at all on this path.

WHAT EACH BUFFER SUBCLASS HOLDS (why the old buffer cannot simply be kept)

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferRGB
      Either a Ref<BitmapTexture> that owns its own glTexImage2D storage (m_textureID == 0),
      or a bare foreign GLuint (m_textureID != 0) owned by whoever created it -- a GstGLMemory
      texture, or a texture imported from a dmabuf. The foreign-id case is the only one the
      old code copied, with BitmapTexture::copyFromExternalTexture() (a glCopyTexSubImage2D
      through a scratch FBO).

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferDMABuf                            (USE(GBM))
      A Ref<DMABufBuffer> holding dup()ed dmabuf fds of the V4L2 CAPTURE buffer plus an
      optional fence fd. On first paint it lazily imports those fds through
      EGL_LINUX_DMA_BUF_EXT into BitmapTextures cached on the DMABufBuffer, which is itself
      cached as qdata on the GstMemory. Retaining it retains the decoder's memory.

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferExternalOES
      A bare GLuint of a GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES texture owned by the GstGLMemory of the
      frame being flushed. Retaining it retains the GstBuffer. It also cannot be copied with
      copyFromExternalTexture(): an external OES texture is not a valid FBO colour attachment,
      it can only be read by a shader that samples samplerExternalOES.

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferYUV
      Up to four plane textures, either bare foreign GLuints from the GstGLMemory or
      BitmapTextures imported from the frame's dmabuf fds. Same ownership problem, and turning
      them into RGB needs the YUV-to-RGB shader pass.

  CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferVideo itself
      An optional GstMappedFrame -- a live GST_MAP_READ (or GST_MAP_GL) mapping of the
      GstBuffer -- plus the inner buffer above.

So on every non-RGB path the flushed frame is decoder-pool memory, and the whole point of the
drop is to let the pool reclaim it. Nothing can be handed over as is; the pixels must be
copied into storage WebKit owns.

APPROACH CHOSEN: one TextureMapper pass into a pooled BitmapTexture -- option (a)

copyBuffer() keeps its existing fast path unchanged, and everything else falls through to a
new copyBufferByPainting(), which renders the buffer once into a BitmapTexture acquired from
BitmapTexturePool and returns a CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferRGB wrapping it. Every subclass
already implements paintToTextureMapper(), so this single path covers DMABuf, ExternalOES,
YUV and the not-yet-uploaded mapped frame without naming any of them -- which is also why the
patch needs no new USE(GBM) / USE(GSTREAMER_GL) guards. The result holds a Ref<BitmapTexture>
and nothing else: no GstBuffer, no fds, no EGLImage, no mapping.

It is reachable at that point in the code. replaceCurrentContentsBufferWithCopy() runs on the
compositing run loop (CoordinatedPlatformLayerBufferProxy dispatches it there), which is the
thread ThreadedCompositor made its GLContext current on, and the pre-existing RGB path already
issues raw GL there. TextureMapper::create() is cheap on an already-used context:
TextureMapperGLData looks up its SharedGLData in a per-platform-context static map, so the
compiled shader programs are shared with the compositor's own TextureMapper; only a small VBO
cache is per instance. A separate instance is used rather than plumbing the compositor's
through CoordinatedPlatformLayer::Client, because beginPainting()/endPainting() save and
restore GL state into the instance and a fresh one provably cannot disturb a frame in flight.

Why the other two options are worse:

  (b) a virtual copyBuffer() per subclass. DMABuf-YUV, YUV and ExternalOES all need a shader
      pass to become RGB, so each override would end up doing exactly what (a) does, three
      times, and each would still need a TextureMapper. More code, same mechanism, and it
      spreads the flush contract across four classes.

  (c) push the sysmem gst_buffer_copy_deep() that flushCurrentBuffer() already makes. It is
      pushable -- pushTextureToCompositor() is right there and setDisplayBuffer() is
      thread-safe -- but the deep copy of a hardware frame is NV12, and the sysmem branch of
      paintToTextureMapper() uploads plane 0 as PixelFormat::BGRA8. The layer would show the
      Y plane as garbage colour instead of black. Fixing that means writing the NV12 upload
      path, which is more work than (a) and duplicates the YUV shader that already exists.
      It also memcpys 12 MB of uncached dmabuf per 4K flush on the streaming thread.

DETAILS THAT ARE EASY TO GET WRONG

  * Orientation flags (ShouldRotateTexture90/180/270, ShouldFlipTexture, ShouldPremultiply)
    are consumed by the paint, so the copy must not carry them or they would be applied twice.
    A quarter turn also transposes the frame, so the copy is allocated at the transposed size
    and painted over its whole extent; TextureMapperLayer paints a contents layer into
    m_state.contentsRect and never consults the buffer's own size, so this is safe.
    ShouldBlend is kept: it describes compositing of the copy, not its texels.

  * TextureMapper::endPainting() restores the bound framebuffer, program, scissor and depth
    state, but NOT the viewport, and BitmapTexture::bindAsSurface() changed it to the copy's
    size. AcceleratedSurface::willRenderFrame() only calls glViewport() when the surface was
    resized, and ThreadedCompositor::paintToCurrentGLContext() reads GL_VIEWPORT in
    beginPainting() to build the clip stack and the projection matrix -- so a stale viewport
    would corrupt every later frame. bindSurface(nullptr) before endPainting() restores it,
    the same way TextureMapperLayer unwinds its intermediate surfaces.

RISK

  * Cost: one full-frame textured quad plus a pooled RGBA texture (33 MB at 4K) per flush,
    on the compositing thread, while dropCurrentBufferWhilePreservingTexture(ShouldWait::Yes)
    blocks the streaming thread. Flushes happen a few times a minute, not per frame. If the
    a540 makes this visible, the cheap knob is to skip the copy above some size and accept
    the black flash for very large frames.
  * The pooled texture is held until the next decoded frame replaces the contents buffer, so
    the pool's high-water mark grows by one frame-sized texture.
  * The rotated-video path (transposed size) is reasoned from the shader's texture-coordinate
    rotation and TextureMapperLayer's use of contentsRect; it is not covered by an existing
    layout test and has not been exercised on device.
  * Unchanged behaviour when the inner buffer is an RGB buffer over a foreign texture id --
    the old code path is byte-for-byte the same -- and on ports whose video layer is always
    RGB the new branch is never reached.

